"lamps"
"These beautifully gilded lamp stands are spaced around the room, providing

ample light.  The stands themselves are wrought of iron, but trimmed in gold,

an odd combination of the lavish and the useful.  Three bars twisted around

one another provide the bases, and gold leaves are wrapped around each bar. 

At the top, a decorated gold plate holds the burning oil lamp, reflecting its

light around the room.

"
E
"fountain"
"This worn marble fountain is showing its age.  What once was probably a

shining white, beautifully carved piece of art is now little more than a place

to store water.  The marble is grey with age, and a ring of black marks the

water line.  Near the wall where the water pours into it, various types of

fungus have taken up residence.  The sides of the fountain have been worn

down, being used as both table and bench.  The only positive thing to be said

about it is the fact that the water it holds is perfectly clear, allowing you

to see the clean marble at the bottom, giving a glimpse of how it once must

have looked.

"
E
"statue"
"This enormous statue of a troll looms over all who enter, including other

trolls.  Cut from a dark granite cut through with streaks of green, the statue

depicts a giant of a troll wielding a great two-handed sword.  The trolls

muscles are enormous, and are all visible as he is clothed in nothing more

than a lion cloth to preserve his modesty, if he has such a thing.  For all

the detail shown in the troll, the true work of art is the sword he wields. 

Every detail of it is perfectly brought to life in stone, down to the

leather-wrapped pommel and etched blade.  There is even a chip in the back

edge of the blade, specifically chiseled away from the grain of the rock so as

not to be mistaken as an accident.

"
E
"plaque"
"Inscribed on the plaque the words: Created with help from: Groob, Affenaut,

and Fenix.  Financial Aid provided by: Groob, Grimslade and Kytorez.  In the

lower left corner of the plaque there is a small green button.

"
